{
  "rationale": "The spec's only hard constraints are throughput >= 1 MSPS and max_abs_err <= 2^-10; area is the sole real cost (objective min luts_plus_ffs, selection min luts_plus_ffs). The fleet data already shows iterative owns the entire merged front (23/23 designs, best feasible luts_plus_ffs 322) while pipelined/pipelined_m are far behind (1581 / 774). iterative also trivially clears the throughput bar: N+3 cycles/result at ~100+ MHz is several MSPS even for N=20, so unrolled_k's only advantage (fewer cycles/result) buys nothing here while it replicates the datapath k times for identical output bits. I therefore spend ~90% of the remaining budget refining/extending the iterative front and only ~10% probing unrolled_k to confirm it is dominated rather than assume it. Accuracy reasoning (from the stated model, not invented numbers): output LSB is 2^-(W-2), so half-LSB rounding error is 2^-(W-1) and the CORDIC residual is ~2^-N; meeting 2^-10 therefore needs W >= ~12 and N >= ~11, putting the low-area corner near W=12-13, N=12-14, while the HV reference point (area 1500, accuracy_bits 10) means the front should also be pushed to larger W/N where accuracy_bits climbs well above 10. angle_guard/frac_guard are included on both sides of 0 because they trade angle-path and x/y fractional width against area and are the cheapest knobs to shave LUTs+FFs at fixed W/N.",
  "families": [
    {
      "why": "Proven front owner: 92/100 feasible, best luts_plus_ffs 322, all 23 merged-front points. Throughput is a non-issue (N+3 cycles/result >> 1 MSPS), so the whole trade-off is area vs accuracy. Ranges straddle the predicted feasibility boundary (W>=12, N>=11) to pin the minimum-area corner, and extend to W,N=20 so the front reaches high accuracy_bits toward the HV reference area of 1500. angle_guard -2..2 and frac_guard 0..2 let the search buy accuracy with the cheapest bits instead of raw W/N; rounding is left free since round costs little area but can rescue the W=12 corner.",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 11, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 9, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -2, "high": 2, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 2, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]}
      ],
      "family": "iterative",
      "budget_share": 0.9
    },
    {
      "why": "Cheap dominance check, not a front candidate. unrolled_k computes bit-identical results for the same (data_width, n_iter, angle_guard, frac_guard, rounding) but chains k micro-rotations per cycle, so it should carry strictly more LUTs at equal accuracy and be dominated in the (luts_plus_ffs, accuracy_bits) plane; its throughput edge is worthless against a 1 MSPS floor. Ranges are deliberately narrow and centred on the region where iterative is cheapest (W 11..16, N 10..16) with small k (2..4) so a handful of evals either confirms domination or reveals a surprise worth a later round.",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 11, "high": 16, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 10, "high": 16, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -1, "high": 2, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 2, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]},
        {"param": "k", "low": 2, "high": 4, "choices": null}
      ],
      "family": "unrolled_k",
      "budget_share": 0.1
    }
  ]
}

{

  "decision": "map_front",
  "next_plan": null,
  "rationale": "Two of the four families (pipelined, pipelined_m) can be ruled out on the evidence: the throughput constraint is trivially met by the cheapest designs (min-area front point runs 13.2 MSPS vs the 1 MSPS requirement, best seen 16.2), so throughput is not binding and a per-cycle pipeline only adds FFs/LUTs that hurt the min-luts_plus_ffs objective and can't help HV (throughput is a constraint, not an axis). That leaves iterative as the family actually on the front, and the exact fit constraint is satisfiable at the low-area end (dw=15/n_iter=12 gives max_abs_err=2^-10.23 <= 2^-10 at luts_plus_ffs=253, which is also the selection-rule pick). The problem is coverage, not feasibility: the feasible front spans luts_plus_ffs 253..355 against an HV reference of 1500, and accuracy_bits only 10.2..14.5 while the registry (data_width up to 28, n_iter up to 30, angle_guard to +4, frac_guard to +4) can clearly push accuracy much higher; data_width on the front stops at 20 of a possible 28. So a large, all-feasible region of the (area, accuracy) plane is unmapped, and HV against (1500, 10) is dominated by the unclaimed high-accuracy part of the curve. This is exactly the map_front trigger, and it also matches the reserved end-of-budget step: NSGA-II over the full iterative ranges (and unrolled_k, which was feasible but never reached the front, as the only other area-competitive family), seeded with the current 18-point front, to extend accuracy upward and fill the area gap up to the 1500 reference."
}

{
  "decision": "refine",
  "next_plan": {
    "families": [
      {
        "budget_share": 0.75,
        "family": "iterative",
        "ranges": [
          {"choices": ["round", "trunc"], "high": null, "low": null, "param": "rounding"},
          {"choices": null, "high": 3, "low": 0, "param": "angle_guard"},
          {"choices": null, "high": 1, "low": 0, "param": "frac_guard"},
          {"choices": null, "high": 17, "low": 13, "param": "data_width"},
          {"choices": null, "high": 17, "low": 10, "param": "n_iter"}
        ]
      ,
      "why": "Holds the selection target under the binding error constraint; only region that has produced the smallest feasible area."}
    ],
    "rationale": "The whole feasible front is iterative-only and the selection rule (min luts_plus_ffs) lands at its low-area corner. That corner is also the riskiest: the winner (dw=15, n_iter=12, ag=2, fg=0, round) sits at 253 LUT+FF and max_abs_err=2^-10.23, only 0.23 bits above the 2^-10 constraint. The front below 300 is thin (253, 263, 278, 296) and the current lowest data_width on it is 15 while the registry allows 8, so a data_width 13-14 design with more iterations may satisfy 2^-10 at lower area but is not yet sampled. Narrowing to data_width 13..17 / n_iter 10..17 / guard 0..3 aims to pin the true low-area minimum before spending the reserved budget on a full front map."
  },
  "rationale": "Budget: 80 of 200 evals left over ~2 rounds. HV jumped +158% this round, so the front is still moving fast and is worth refining rather than freezing now. Evidence: (1) 0% of evals violate throughput (need >=1 MSPS, best seen 16.2), so throughput is free and only area/accuracy matter; (2) 29% violate the error bound, and the selecting design is only 0.23 bits clear of it - the low-area corner is where refinement pays and where a missed smaller design would directly change the selected answer; (3) unrolled_k is strictly worse on area (best feasible luts_plus_ffs=357 vs iterative 253) and is not on the front, so it does not need budget; (4) the front already spans accuracy 10.2..23.2 against HV ref 10 and 253..617 against HV ref 1500, so coverage of the high-accuracy tail is adequate - the missing coverage is sub-253 area. After this refinement, the final round should be used to map the full iterative front (the only family on the front) seeded with the refined front."
}

{
  "decision": "stop",
  "next_plan": null
  ,
  "rationale": "Budget is exhausted (200/200 evals, round 3 of 4) and the rules make this the final round, so only stop/infeasible are available. Infeasible is ruled out: the feasible front has 23 designs, throughput constraint is met by 100% of evaluations (best seen 16.2 MSPS vs the 1 MSPS floor) and max_abs_err reaches 2^-23.18, far below the 2^-10 limit (best feasible = 1.05e-07). The front also spans both objectives well: luts_plus_ffs 253..617 against the HV reference 1500, accuracy_bits 10.2..23.2 against the HV reference 10, with data_width 15..27 covered. The spec's selection rule (min luts_plus_ffs) is satisfied directly by the front's leading point — iterative [W=15, N=12, angle_guard=2, frac_guard=0, trunc/round=round] at luts_plus_ffs=253 (luts=161, ffs=93), throughput 13.2 MSPS >= 1, max_abs_err 0.000835 = 2^-10.23 <= 2^-10 — i.e. a design that meets every constraint while minimising the area proxy. The last round produced only +0.1% hypervolume, so returns are diminishing and further exploration is not warranted."
}
